We’ll use the formula for excess pressure inside a **spherical drop**:

\[
\Delta P = \frac{2T}{r}
\]

Given: 
- Radius \(r = 2\,\text{mm} = 2 \times 10^{-3}\,\text{m}\) 
- Surface tension \(T = 73 \times 10^{-3}\,\text{N/m}\) (this is the usual value for water)

\[
\Delta P = \frac{2 \times 73 \times 10^{-3}}{2 \times 10^{-3}}
= \frac{146 \times 10^{-3}}{2 \times 10^{-3}}
= 73\ \text{Pa}
\]

**Answer: The pressure inside is 73 Pa more than the pressure outside.**

